+++ b/.cartridge/docs/fact-provenance.md
@@ -0,0 +1,28 @@
+# Reading fact provenance
+
+Native `query`, `get`, batch ID lookup and compact lookup expose the same `provenance` object. Both local ownership and attachment to an existing daemon use the shared projections. Existing source links remain in `source` with `scheme`, `object_id`, `section` and `url`.
+
+The object has schema `memory.provenance.v1`. `status` is `active` or `superseded`, `review` is `active` or `pending`, and `superseded_by` is the recorded successor's exact ID or null. These labels report stored lifecycle decisions; they do not establish that a claim is true.
+
+| Field | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| `created_at_ms` | Recorded entity creation time, when present. |
+| `updated_at_ms` | Recorded entity update time, including existing confidence updates. |
+| `observed_at_ms` | Null: there is no separate stored clock proving when the external source was observed. |
+| `valid_from_ms`, `valid_to_ms` | Recorded world-validity interval. |
+| `invalidated_at_ms` | Recorded transaction time at which the revision was invalidated. |
+| `valid_until_ms` | Retention deadline; separate from lifecycle supersession. |
+
+Times are signed Unix milliseconds, rounded down. A nanosecond before the epoch becomes `-1`; an absent clock remains null. Clocks that exceed the signed integer range become null and are named in the bounded `unrepresentable_clocks` list. No clock is replaced by query time, access time, filesystem time, or another date.
+
+`freshness: "recorded"` means a creation or update time is available. It makes no claim that the source was recently checked. `unknown` means neither time is stored, which is normal for legacy facts. `unrepresentable` means creation/update clocks exist but none fit the wire range. Validity or retention dates alone cannot establish creation/update freshness. Existing top-level time fields retain their earlier seconds-based contract.
+
+Edges keep their numeric `kind` and add `kind_label`: `similarity`, `provenance`, `question`, `spawn`, `supersedes`, `ratification`, `rephrase`, or `deleted`. Every returned edge names its exact `id`, `from`, and `to`. A `supersedes` edge points from the newer revision to the older one. `rephrase` records a proposed wording; it does not prove a conflict. `deleted` records source deletion and can have an empty target. Memory reports these stored relations without comparing claims or fetching sources to infer new conflicts.
+
+A default ranked query still excludes superseded revisions. Request `include_history: true` to include the existing revision history, or use `get` with either exact ID. Missing referenced evidence returns the existing missing-ID error; the edge itself does not guarantee the target remains available.
+
+Ranked rows include at most 12 eligible edges in score/ID order. `edges_total`, `edges_more`, and `edges_scope: "enriched_or_lifecycle"` state the scope and whether eligible edges were omitted. Eligible means an enriched explanation or a recorded supersedes/rephrase/deleted relation, even when its text is empty. An empty edge sample does not establish that no real-world conflict exists.
+
+Direct `get` retains its existing indexed-edge pagination (`edge_limit`, `edge_cursor`) and counts all its indexed edges. Compact lookup retains provenance and edge labels/endpoints while omitting edge text and limiting fact text. Cold direct reads retain provenance and the cold flag, with their existing lack of resident graph edges.
+
+Projection does not write entity metadata, fetch external sources, run inference, or change formats. Existing query access telemetry still belongs to the owning writer. Attachment failures remain explicit, with no local fallback or second writer. Removing these additive fields requires no store rewrite.
